76</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>A24</fpage>(Abstr.)</citation></ref><ref id="ShyngandNichols1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shyng</surname><given-names>S.-L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Nichols</surname><given-names>C.G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Membrane phospholipid control of nucleotide sensitivity of K<sub>ATP</sub> channels</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>282</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>1138</fpage><lpage>1141</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9804554</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Shyngetal1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shyng</surname><given-names>S.-L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferrrigni</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Nichols</surname><given-names>C.G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Control of rectification and gating of cloned K<sub>ATP</sub> channels by the Kir6.2 subunit</article-title><source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source><volume>110</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>141</fpage><lpage>153</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9236207</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Terzicetal1994"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Terzic</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Findlay</surname><given-names>I.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hosoya</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kurachi</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Dualistic behavior of ATP-sensitive K<sup>+</sup> channels toward intracellular nucleotide diphosphates</article-title><source>Neuron</source><volume>12</volume><year>1994</year><fpage>1049</fpage><lpage>1058</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8185943</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Trappetal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Trapp</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Proks</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tucker</surname><given-names>S.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ashcroft</surname><given-names>F.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Molecular analysis of ATP-sensitive K channel gating and implications for channel inhibition by ATP</article-title><source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source><volume>112</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>333</fpage><lpage>349</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9725893</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Tuckeretal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tucker</surname><given-names>S.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gribble</surname><given-names>F.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Proks</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Trapp</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ryder</surname><given-names>T.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Haug</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Reimann</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ashcroft</surname><given-names>F.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Molecular determinants of KATP channel inhibition by ATP</article-title><source>EMBO (Eur. Mol. Biol. Organ.) J.</source><volume>17</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>3290</fpage><lpage>3296</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="Tuckeretal1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tucker</surname><given-names>S.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gribble</surname><given-names>F.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhao</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Trapp</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ashcroft</surname><given-names>F.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Truncation of Kir6.2 produces ATP-sensitive K channels in the absence of the sulfonylurea receptor</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>387</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>179</fpage><lpage>182</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9144288</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Xieetal1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Xie</surname><given-names>L.H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Takano</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kakei</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Okamura</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Noma</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Wortmannin, an inhibitor of phosphatidylinositol kinases, blocks the MgATP-dependent recovery of Kir6.2/SUR2A channels</article-title><source>J. Physiol.</source><volume>514</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>655</fpage><lpage>665</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9882737</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> GFP, green fluorescent protein&#x0003b; PIP<sub>2</sub>, phosphatidylinositol bisphosphate.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Spontaneous rundown of Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channels in excised inside-out patches. (A) Time course of rundown after patch excision into a MgATP-free solution with a Ca<sup>2+</sup> concentration of 2 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;7</sup> M, demonstrating an initial fast phase followed by a slow phase. The dotted line represents zero current, and downward deflections represent inward currents. Holding potential was &#x02212;10 mV. (B) ATP sensitivity before rundown. Inward current was progressively suppressed by increasing ATP concentrations. The IC<sub>50</sub> for channel inhibition by ATP was near 100 &#x003bc;M for this patch. In the presence of 400 &#x003bc;M ATP, addition of 300 &#x003bc;M MgADP caused a robust stimulation of channel activity, indicating functional coupling between Kir6.2G and SUR1. (C) The same inside-out patch after prolonged rundown, showing increased sensitivity to ATP with an IC<sub>50</sub> near 12.5 &#x003bc;M, and loss of stimulation by MgADP, indicating functional uncoupling of Kir6.2G and SUR1. (D) ATP dose&#x02013;response curves before (&#x025b4;) and after (&#x02022;) spontaneous prolonged rundown. Data points are the mean &#x000b1; SD of six values from different patches. Current amplitude was normalized to the current measured in the same patch in the absence of nucleotide. Lines are best fits to a Hill equation: <italic>I</italic>/<italic>I</italic><sub>max</sub> = 1/{1 + ([ATP]/IC<sub>50</sub>)<sup>n</sup>}, with the IC<sub>50</sub> values listed and <italic>n</italic> = 1.1&#x02013;1.2.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Spontaneous rundown of Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channels and the effects of MgADP. (A) Inward current from an inside-out patch excised into bath solution containing 150 &#x003bc;M MgADP, demonstrating absence of fast rundown until MgADP was removed. (B and C) Representative data from inside-out patches showing inward currents with little rundown that are suppressed by increasing ATP concentrations with and without 200 &#x003bc;M MgADP, respectively. In the absence of MgADP (C), the IC<sub>50</sub> for inhibition by ATP was near 150 &#x003bc;M&#x0003b; in the presence of MgADP (B), the IC<sub>50</sub> value was near 400 &#x003bc;M. (D) Correlation between channel ATP sensitivity with and without 200 &#x003bc;M MgADP. Patches were grouped in two categories. In the first category, which exhibited a pronounced fast phase of rundown, the average IC<sub>50</sub> for inhibition by ATP was 30 &#x000b1; 14 &#x003bc;M in the absence of MgADP and 152 &#x000b1; 61 &#x003bc;M with 200 &#x003bc;M MgADP. In the second category, with little or no fast rundown, the average IC<sub>50</sub> for inhibition by ATP was 171 &#x000b1; 46 &#x003bc;M in the absence of MgADP and 380 &#x000b1; 50 &#x003bc;M with 200 &#x003bc;M MgADP. The dotted line was fit using the equation <italic>y</italic> = 1/{1 + (<italic>k</italic>/[ATP])<sup>n</sup>}, which yielded <italic>k</italic> = 74 &#x003bc;M and <italic>n</italic> = 0.96. The latter suggests that one MgADP is sufficient to shift the ATP sensitivity.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown and reactivation in Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channels. (A) Brief application of 10 &#x003bc;M Ca<sup>2+</sup>, for &#x0223c;1 min, to the cytoplasmic surface of an excised inside-out patch rapidly inhibited channel activity, which recovered only minimally after removal of Ca<sup>2+</sup>. Subsequent application of 100 &#x003bc;M MgATP inhibited the residual current, but reactivated the channels, as evidenced by the increase in current after MgATP removal. The currents are inward, with the zero current level indicated by the dashed line. (B and C) ATP sensitivity in a representative inside-out patch before (B) and after (C) exposure to 10 &#x003bc;M Ca<sup>2+</sup> for 1 min. Inward current was progressively suppressed by increasing [ATP] as indicated. The IC<sub>50</sub> after Ca<sup>2+</sup> exposure decreased from near 100 to &#x0223c;15 &#x003bc;M. Stimulation by 200 &#x003bc;M MgADP remained potent after short exposure to Ca<sup>2+</sup> causing rundown. (D) Plot of channel ATP sensitivity with and without 200 &#x003bc;M MgADP, before (&#x02022;) and after (&#x025cb;) short exposure (&#x0003c;1 min) to Ca<sup>2+</sup>. Brief exposure to Ca<sup>2+</sup> steepens the relationship as the ATP sensitivity increases without major decrease in MgADP-dependent reactivation. After long exposure to Ca<sup>2+</sup>, MgADP no longer reactivates the channel and the curve dropped below that obtained before Ca<sup>2+</sup> application (not shown).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Regulation of Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channel sensitivity to ATP and MgADP by PIP<sub>2</sub>. (A and B) Representative inward currents from excised inside-out patches exposed to 50 &#x003bc;M PIP<sub>2</sub> after rundown caused by brief (A) or prolonged (B) exposure to 10 &#x003bc;M Ca<sup>2+</sup>. Dashed lines indicate zero current level. PIP<sub>2</sub> led to full recovery of channel activity after brief Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown, but minimal recovery after prolonged (&#x0003e;5 min) exposure to Ca<sup>2+</sup>. (C and D) ATP sensitivity in representative inside-out patches after fast spontaneous rundown, and before (C) or after (D) exposure to PIP<sub>2</sub>. Inward currents were progressively suppressed by increasing ATP concentrations, with the IC<sub>50</sub> after PIP<sub>2</sub> increasing from near 25 to &#x0003e;250 &#x003bc;M. Treatment with PIP<sub>2</sub> caused loss of MgADP-dependent reactivation when 300 &#x003bc;M MgADP was applied in the presence ATP. (E) ATP dose&#x02013;response curves for inside-out patches after brief Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown (&#x02022;) and reactivation by PIP<sub>2</sub> (&#x025aa;), showing a marked increase in the IC<sub>50</sub>. Also shown (&#x025b4;) is the ATP dose&#x02013;response curve after prolonged Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown and subsequent exposure to PIP<sub>2</sub> for &#x0003e;10 min. Although PIP<sub>2</sub> did not significantly increase current amplitude (B), it markedly increased the IC<sub>50</sub> to the millimolar range. Data points are the mean &#x000b1; SD of three to five values from different patches. Current amplitude was normalized to the current measured in the same patch in the absence of nucleotide. Lines are best fits to a Hill equation, as described in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Fig. 1</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>PIP<sub>2</sub> affects Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced channel inhibition. (A) Data similar to that in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">Fig. 4</xref> A, where Ca<sup>2+</sup>-inhibited channels are reactivated by application of 50 &#x003bc;M PIP<sub>2</sub>. (B) After reactivation by PIP<sub>2</sub>, subsequent application of 10 &#x003bc;M Ca<sup>2+</sup> for up to 10 min blocked inward current, but did not cause rundown, since the current recovered once Ca<sup>2+</sup> was removed.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Protein kinase inhibitors affect Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channel regulation by ATP and MgADP. (A) Application of 100 &#x003bc;M H-7, a nonselective protein kinase inhibitor, to the cytoplasmic surface of an excised inside-out patch modestly accelerated rundown, which was stabilized when H-7 was removed. After H-7 removal, channel activity was completely suppressed by 100 &#x003bc;M MgATP, but did not recover beyond the rundown level when MgATP was removed, indicating that the protein kinase inhibitor has irreversible effects. The superimposed trace from another patch shows that 1 &#x003bc;M okadaic acid (OA) prevented rundown by H-7. Currents are inward, with zero-current levels indicated by the dashed line. (B) Similar results were obtained using the selective PKC inhibitor peptide 19-36 (5 &#x003bc;M) in place of H-7. (C) ATP sensitivity in a representative inside-out patch after treatment with H-7. Inward current was progressively suppressed by increasing ATP concentrations, with the IC<sub>50</sub> well below 25 &#x003bc;M. Application of 300 &#x003bc;M MgADP did not significantly stimulate channel activity after H-7, indicating functional uncoupling from SUR1. (D) Representative patch excised into MgADP-containing bath solution to prevent fast rundown. This panel shows that application of the selective PKC inhibitor peptide 19-36 (5 &#x003bc;M) induced rundown and uncoupling from SUR1 even in the presence of MgADP. In this case, inhibition by ADP became manifest due to functional uncoupling of Kir6.2 and SUR1, as evidenced by MgADP removal and reapplication at the end of the trace. (E) ATP dose&#x02013;response curves for inside-out patches before (&#x025b4;) and after (&#x02022;) H-7 induced rundown, showing a marked decrease in the IC<sub>50</sub> after H-7. Data points are the mean &#x000b1; SD of four to five values from different patches. Current amplitude was normalized to the current measured in the same patch in the absence of nucleotide. Lines are best fits to a Hill equation, as described in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Fig. 1</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Channel phosphorylation and MgADP control Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channel regulation by PIP<sub>2</sub>. (A) A representative patch excised into bath solution containing MgADP to prevent the fast phase of spontaneous rundown. Exposure to 50 &#x003bc;M PIP<sub>2</sub> had no effect on channel activity in ATP-free solution. (B) In the presence of MgADP, however, when rundown was induced by application of the selective PKC inhibitor peptide 19-36 (5 &#x003bc;M), application of PIP<sub>2</sub> reactivated the rundown channels. Currents were inward in both traces, with the zero current levels indicated by dashed lines.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown is an intrinsic property of the Kir6.2 subunit of the K<sub>ATP</sub> channel. (A) Representative currents from an excised inside-out patch containing homomeric Kir6.2-GFP channels. Application of 10 &#x003bc;M Ca<sup>2+</sup> induced rapid channel block, which persisted after Ca<sup>2+</sup> removal, indicating rundown. (B and C) ATP sensitivity in representative inside-out patches before (B) and after (C) exposure to Ca<sup>2+</sup>. Inward current was progressively suppressed by increasing ATP concentrations, with the IC<sub>50</sub> after Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown decreasing from near 200 to &#x0003c;50 &#x003bc;M. (E) ATP dose&#x02013;response curves for inside-out patches before (&#x025be;) after (&#x02022;) Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown, showing a marked decrease in the IC<sub>50</sub>. Data points are the mean &#x000b1; SD of three to four values from different patches. Channel activity was normalized to the activity measured in the same patch in the absence of nucleotide. Lines are best fits to a Hill equation, as described in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Fig. 1</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f8"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Reactivation of homomeric Kir6.2-GFP channels by PIP<sub>2</sub> after rundown induced by Ca<sup>2+</sup> and protein kinase inhibitors. (A) Representative excised inside-out patch showing channel reactivation by PIP<sub>2</sub> after rundown due to brief Ca<sup>2+</sup> application. Currents are inward, with the zero current level indicated by the dashed line. (B) ATP dose&#x02013;response curves for inside-out patches after Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced rundown before (&#x02022;) and after (&#x025be;) reactivation by PIP<sub>2</sub>, showing a marked increase in the IC<sub>50</sub>. Data points are the mean &#x000b1; SD of three to four values from different patches. Channel activity was normalized to the activity measured in the same patch in the absence of nucleotide. Lines are best fits to a Hill equation, as described in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Fig. 1</xref>. (C) Currents from a representative inside-out patch demonstrating that nonselective protein kinase inhibition with 50 &#x003bc;M H-7 accelerated rundown of Kir6.2-GFP channels, similar to Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channels. (D and E) Currents from representative inside-out patches, in which rundown was induced by the combined effects of the selective protein kinase inhibitor, peptide 19-36 (5 &#x003bc;M), and brief application of Ca<sup>2+</sup> (10 &#x003bc;M). PIP<sub>2</sub> failed to significantly reactivate Kir6.2-GFP channels (D), but was effective at reactivating Kir6.2-GFP+SUR1 channels (E).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f9"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F10"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Different effects of reactivation by MgATP and PIP<sub>2</sub> on Kir6.2-GFP single channel kinetics. (A) Representative single channel current recordings from an excised inside-out patch at a holding potential of &#x02212;25 mV after prolonged spontaneous rundown left only two channels in the patch. Recordings were obtained before (top) and after (bottom) application of 100 &#x003bc;M MgATP to reactivate the channels. Downward deflections represent inward currents. (B) Open-time histograms corresponding to the single channel currents in A, fit with two exponentials (dotted lines). (Top) Before MgATP: &#x003c4;<sub>fast</sub> = 0.7 ms and &#x003c4;<sub>slow</sub> = 2.7 ms&#x0003b; (bottom) after MgATP: &#x003c4;<sub>fast</sub> = 0.9 ms and &#x003c4;<sub>slow</sub> = 3 ms. (E, a) MgATP did not significantly affect the two time constants. The tables in B list the amplitudes of the two exponentials components in arbitrary units. MgATP increased the number of fast openings by 3.5-fold and the number of long openings by 1.25-fold. (C and D) Corresponding single channel traces and open time histograms from another inside-out patch after prolonged rundown, before (top) and after (bottom) reactivation by 50 &#x003bc;M PIP<sub>2</sub>. In contrast to MgATP, PIP<sub>2</sub> selectively increased the number of long burst of openings (threefold), while increasing only very slightly the number of short openings (1.2-fold). In addition, PIP<sub>2</sub> increased the duration of the bursts of openings (&#x003c4;<sub>slow</sub> = 2.2 and 4.2 ms before and after PIP<sub>2</sub>, respectively). (E, b) Graphs showing averaged &#x003c4;'s and increased number of events in three patches, after MgATP (left) and PIP<sub>2</sub> (right).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f10"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F11"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>Model for K<sub>ATP</sub> channel regulation by PIP<sub>2</sub>, MgADP, and phosphorylation. In this model, channels are formed by Kir6.2 and SUR1 subunits, which have regulatory sites for PIP<sub>2</sub> and MgADP, respectively. (A) States 1 and 2 represent two physiological states in intact cells, which differ by their PIP<sub>2</sub> regulatory site occupancy. The distribution between these two states is determined by the cellular level of PIP<sub>2</sub>. The other four states result from rundown that occurs via alternate pathways (A&#x02013;C) after patch excision. States 1&#x02013;3 have similar high <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub> and occupancy of one or both regulatory sites causes maximum channel activation (high <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub>). Only State 4, which has both regulatory sites free, exhibits a low <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub>. The low <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub> of State 4 accounts, at least in part, for its high ATP sensitivity, and the high <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub> in the other states accounts for their low ATP sensitivity (see text for explanation). After dissociation of MgADP (pathway A), the transition from State 1 to State 3 has little effect on channel activity, since both states have similar <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub>, only transition from State 2 to 4 leads to fast rundown. Transition from State 1 to 3 and 4 leads to slow rundown due to loss of PIP<sub>2</sub> (pathway C). In this case, the loss of PIP<sub>2</sub> (State 3 to 4) is much slower than the loss of MgADP (State 1 to 3) and is, therefore, the rate-limiting step. Another form of rundown occurs as a result of dephosphorylation (pathway B). Dephosphorylation induces loss of MgADP-dependent reactivation of ATP-inhibited channels and is taken as evidence for functional uncoupling of SUR1 and Kir6.2. In the model, this is represented by the transitions from State 3 to 5 and from State 4 to 6. The effect of dephosphorylation-induced uncoupling on <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub> and ATP sensitivity may be only apparent in the absence of PIP<sub>2</sub>-dependent regulation (transitions from State 2 to 4 to 6) and have minor effects when PIP<sub>2</sub> regulates channel activity (transitions from State 1 to 3 to 5). Dephosphorylation may also have a long-term effect and cause deactivation of a PIP<sub>2</sub> regulatory site, facilitating the component of slow rundown related to loss of PIP<sub>2</sub> (pathway C). Lastly, not shown in this scheme, further rundown of States 5 and 6 leads to complete and irreversible channel closure, possibly after extensive Kir6.2 dephosphorylation. (B) In addition to decreasing the sensitivity to ATP by increasing <italic>P</italic><sub>o</sub>, PIP<sub>2</sub> may affect directly the binding of ATP to Kir6.2. This dramatic effect (sensitivity to ATP as low as 5 mM) occurs after prolonged exposure to PIP<sub>2</sub> and is independent of the rundown state of the channel. Under physiological conditions, such a decrease in binding affinity may be restricted to States 2&#x02013;5, so that K<sub>ATP</sub> channels remain sensitive to adenine nucleotides.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8200.f11"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>